theastern University, Boston, MA 02115, USA\nE-mail: wanunu@neu.edu\nReceived 21 October 2014, revised 4 December 2014\nAccepted for publication 9 December 2014\nPublished 2 February 2015\nAbstract\nNanopores are being hailed as a potential next-generation DNA sequencer that could provide\ncheap, high-throughput DNA analysis. In this review we present a detailed summary of the\nvarious sensing techniques being investigated for use in DNA sequencing and mapping\napplications. A crucial impasse to the success of nanopores as a reliable DNA analysis tool is the\nfast and stochastic nature of DNA translocation. We discuss the incorporation of biological\nmotors to step DNA through a pore base-by-base, as well as the many experimental\nmodiﬁcations attempted for the purpose of slowing and controlling DNA transport.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMolecular ping-pong Game of Life on a two-dimensional DNA origami array. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26078341/)\nciteturn125search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago;   * DOI: 10.1098/rsta.2014.0215 ...   * PII: rsta.2014.0215\n# Molecular ping-pong Game of Life on a two-dimensional DNA origami array.\n\nPhilosophical transactions. Series A, Mathematical, physical, and engineering sciences\n\nPMID: 26078341\n\nPublication date: 2015 Jul 28\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: Philos Trans A Math Phys Eng Sci\n\nJournal country: England\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 101133385\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 1364-503X\n\nISSN: 1364-503X\n\nVolume: 373\n\nIssue: 2046\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.1098/rsta.2014.0215\n  * PII: 20140215\n  * PII: rsta.2014.0215\n  * PMC: PMC7398061\n\n## Authors\n\n  * N Jonoska\n  * N C Seeman\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Department of Mathematics and Statistics, University of South Florida, Tampa, FL, USA jonoska@mail.usf.edu.\n  * Department of Chemistry, New York University, New York, NY, USA.\n\n## 